{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787164000,"nonce":"A7F3JclQBsfptaQ22ra9RaXoTQg=","message":"**Fixed in `34fbd74`, on `main`.**\n\n`truncateAt` now derives its cut from the suffix it actually built. `sectionTruncateAt = 2900` and `maxFieldText - 120` are both deleted — there is nothing left to tune and no URL length can overrun. `ellipsis` and `seeFullDetail` are named so the arithmetic that used to be a magic allowance can be derived rather than asserted.\n\n**The no-useful-head case, recorded as the ticket asks.** The rule is that **the text must be at least as long as the pointer to the rest of it**: if the link suffix would take more than half the budget, the link is dropped and a bare ellipsis remains. Dropping the link is the right half to sacrifice, and `truncateRunes` in the same file already made that call for the same reason — *\"there is no 'view in oto' link here: these strings are too short to hang one off, and every card already carries the group link.\"* The link is duplicated elsewhere on the card; the alert text is not duplicated anywhere.\n\nA budget smaller than the ellipsis itself now returns empty rather than overrunning. An empty text object is refused by the V-checks (`TestAnEmptyTextObjectIsRefusedRatherThanShippedAsAnEmptyBlock`), so an impossible budget surfaces loudly instead of shipping an over-length block — which is the exact failure this function exists to prevent.\n\n**Tests — `truncation_test.go`, and it is the directory's only INTERNAL test, on purpose.** The claim is about `truncateSection`/`truncateField` across URL lengths, not about one card that happens to exercise them; an external test can only reach them through a Reason whose body grows past 3 000 bytes, which pins the card and not the bound. Four tests:\n\n- never exceeds its limit for 23 URL lengths from 33 to 5 900 bytes. **84 is in the list and is the regression**: the new arithmetic gives suffix 113, cut 2 887, total exactly 3 000, where the old gave 2 900 + 113 = 3 013.\n- the link is kept at a 1 471-byte URL and dropped at 1 472 — `maxSectionText/2 − 29` — so the half-budget ruling is pinned at the byte, and dropping it is asserted to return the budget to the TEXT rather than merely shorten the result.\n- no dangling markup at any URL length from 33 to 1 600 in steps of 7, over a body that is itself full of links so the cut lands inside one: valid UTF-8, balanced `\u003c`/`\u003e`, within the limit.\n- inert below the limit, including exactly AT it.\n\n**No fixture moved.** Every checked-in capture is far below 3 000 bytes, so the changed cut position is unreachable for them — confirmed by the golden tests passing unmodified rather than by assuming it.\n\n**Gate:** the eleven `ci` recipes at `34fbd74`, `dirty_before 0` / `dirty_after 0` so it provably measured the committed tree. Nine green; `lint-vocabulary` exactly 60 and one `--- FAIL` (`TestNoStateHueInChrome`), both the measured baseline. Zero drift in either direction.\n\n⚠️ **The 71-byte threshold in this ticket's body was mine, computed from the constants rather than observed** — the red team's probe used a ~4 000-byte URL and made this look like it needed an absurd value. The arithmetic said an ordinary corporate hostname would do it, and the test at n=84 is that claim executed.\n\n**Related and now also fixed:** `3f5e952` makes a malformed `base_url` illegal at boot. Neither subsumes the other — this one makes an over-long URL degrade instead of killing the delivery; that one stops the URL being wrong in the first place.","files":null}]}